          synth - IF synthesizer (DBBC3 racks)

            Syntax:     synth=loX,freq,enable,check
                        synth=
                        synth=loX
            Response:   synth/loX,freq,enable,att,mode,lock

Settable Parameters:

            loX       Synthesizer for the LO (IF) to set/read: 'loa', 'lob',
                      ..., up to the last IF in the system ('loh' is the last
                      possible), or "next' to read the synthesizer after the
                      previous one read (wrapping after the last). 'next'
                      cannot be used with 'force' (for the 'check' parameter).
                      No default.

            freq      frequency in MHZ for the synthesizer. No default unless
                      'ifX' and 'lo=loX,...' have been setup or 'enable' is
                      'off'. If 'ifX' input '2' is selected and 'loX' has been
                      defined with 'lo=...' the frequency set for 'lo=loX,...'
                      is the default. If 'ifX' input '1' or enable is 'off'),
                      the default is "undefined." For Monitoring and Checking
                      (see below in Comments), the value is shown in
                      parentheses if 'enable' is 'off'.

            enable    'off' or 'on'. No default unless 'ifX' has been setup.
                      If 'ifX' input '2' is selected the default is 'on';
                      input '1', 'off'.

            check     Either 'check' or 'force'. Default is 'check'. 'force'
                      cannot used with 'next' (for the 'loX' parameter). If
                      'check', the device is checked to see of it it agrees
                      with the commanded parameters and that 'mode' is 'CW"
                      and that the status is 'locked'.

Monitor-only Parameters:

            att       attenuation, '0.0' to '31.5' dB in '0.5' dB steps.

            mode      'CW', 'SWEEP', or 'LIST' (or 'unknown', if not one of
                      three preceding values). For 'check', any value except
                      'CW' will generate an error report.

            lock      'locked' or 'unlocked' (or 'unknown', if not one of two
                      preceding values). For 'check', an error report will be
                      generated if the value is not 'locked'.

Comments:
CAUTION: This command takes about two seconds per IF to execute and will
interfere with multicast transmission.

In addition to the normal 'setting' and 'monitoring' forms of most commands,
this one has an additional 'checking' form. The details of these forms are
summarized below.

  Setting -- The setting form is invoked by using a literal 'force' for the
             'check' parameter and will command the device. The values of
             'freq' and 'enable' also set the FS model (and hence the values
             for '*' in subsequent commands) for that IF. Using '*' if the
             synthesizer values for that IF has not previously been defined is
             an error for setting. Using 'next' is not permitted.

  Checking -- The 'checking' form, which is the default, is invoked by using a
              literal 'check' (or null value, i.e., the default) for the
              'check' parameter. In this case, the values of 'freq' and
              'enable' are compared to the device's configuration. The value
              for 'freq' is not checked if the device state of 'enable' is
              'off'. Additionally, the mode and lock status are checked. Any
              deviations from what is expected are reported as errors. Just
              like for setting, the values of 'freq' and 'enable' set the FS
              model for that IF.

              With care, 'next' can be specified for the IF for checking,
              typically with '*' for 'freq' and 'enable'.

              If a specific IF is used, the corresponding LO value
              ('lo=loX,...'), has not been set, and the 'freq' and 'enable'
              parameters are default (null), the command is a no-op.

              Similarly, if a specific IF is used and the synthesizer values
              have not been set, using '*' for any other parameter makes the
              command a no-op. See comments below about using a specific IF.

  Monitoring -- The monitor forms are 'synth' (for all IFs) or 'synth=loX'
                (with no following parameters). This form does not set the FS
                model or report errors in the device configuration. Using
                'next' in place of 'loX' is permitted.

Using 'synth=' clears the FS model for all synthesizers. It should be used
before setting the synthesizers for a mode so that unused synthesizers don't
have their definitions carried over from a previous mode.

The IF for 'next' is the next one after the last IF monitored or checked
command. In other words, it is shared between monitor and checking. It
advances even if there is an communication error so that it doesn't become
stuck on one IF.

For a specific IF, i.e., not 'next', if the 'freq' and 'mode' parameters are
null (default) and the LO frequency has not been defined for this IF
('lo=...'), the command is a no-op. This allows which synthesizer are used to
be controlled by which IFs are in use without modifying the commands in the
setup procedure. The synthesizer setup should start with a 'synth=' command,
followed by commands similar to:

  synth=loa,,,check

should be used all IFs in the system, whether being used or not. See more
about the 'dbbc3_synth' procedure below.

For a specific IF, i.e., not 'next', if synthesizer values have not been
previously set and '*' is used for any of them, the command is a no-op. This
allows a fixed procedure to check all the synthesizers in use. Commands
similar to

  synth=loa,*,*,check

should be used for all IFs in the system. There will only be output for
synthesizers with defined setups. If no synthesizer have been defined, there
will be no output. You can add a command at the end of the procedure to get
some feedback when it is done:

  op_stream="Synthesizer checking complete. Only defined synthesizers were checked."

An example 'synth_check' procedure for eight IFs is included in the example
procedure libraries 'd3fbstation.prc' and 'dbbc3tt.prc' in
'/usr2/fs/st,default/proc'.

You can also monitor (not check) all the synthesizers with a
'synth' command or a specific one with 'synth=loX'

If 'next' is used with 'check', IFs that have not been setup will be skipped.
This allows the use of:

  synth=next,*,*,check

to cyclically check each setup IF, one at time. See more about the 'checkXXX'
procedure below.

If 'enable' is 'off' no freq value is sent to the device.

The frequency sent to, and read from, the device is half the value set/shown
in this command, 'freq'. The latter is the effective frequency of the device.

The 'att' value is set in the boot configuration files and should have been
determined in the laboratory before shipping.

The 'synth' command is intended to be used in the 'dbbc3_synth' SNAP procedure
in the 'station' library to check that the configuration of the synthesizer
agrees with the settings of the 'lo=loX,...' and 'ifX=...' commands for each
IF. It is called by drudg generated setup procedure for DBBC3 racks. The
procedure should contain:

  synth=
  synth=loa,,,check

With the 'loa' command repeated for each additional IF up to the number of IFs
that your system has (as set in the 'dbbc3.ctl' control file). For IFs that
don't have a first LO defined (with 'lo=...'), the command for that
synthesizer will be a no-op.

If appropriate, you can use non-default values for 'freq' and 'enable'. If
need be, you can use 'force' to command instead of just checking. In that
case, you should follow that group of commands with 'check' commands for all
the IFs in your system. For example for 'loa' you would use:

   synth=loa,*,*,check

Because this command takes about two seconds to monitor/check each IF and
interferes with multicast transmission, the IF can be specified as 'next' to
provide progressive checking of one IF at a time in, for example, your station
'checkXXX' procedure (which normally includes the scan check) to check the
synthesizers cyclically. The form should be:

  synth=next,*,*,check

Only IFs with defined synthesizer setups will be checked. Using asterisks
(using '*' is recommended to allow it to work for previously set (or checked)
values of 'freq' and 'enable', whether defaults or not.

If you use this command in 'checkxxx', you should verify first that the two
seconds required doesn't delay schedule execution.
